PARSING WITH JPARSEC
====================
 
THE CORE IDEA
-------------
JParsec is a combinator-based parsing library. You build complex parsers by
combining small primitive parsers using operators. Every parser has type
Parser<'a>, meaning it consumes some input and produces a value of type 'a.
 
 
PRIMITIVES
----------
pchar 'x'       matches exactly the character 'x'
pstring "PUSH"  matches exactly the string "PUSH"
pint32          matches a signed integer
satisfy f       matches any single char where f c = true
many p          matches p zero or more times, returns 'a list
many1 p         matches p one or more times, returns 'a list
 
 
COMBINING PARSERS - THE OPERATORS
----------------------------------
p1 >>. p2      run p1 then p2, keep p2's result (discard left)
p1 .>> p2      run p1 then p2, keep p1's result (discard right)
p1 .>>. p2     run both, keep both as a tuple
p1 <|> p2      try p1, if it fails try p2
p  |>> f       run p, apply f to the result (map)
choice [p1; p2; p3]   try each in order, take the first that succeeds
 
The mnemonic for >>. and .>> is: the dot is on the side you keep.
 
 
FORWARDED REFERENCES - FOR RECURSIVE GRAMMARS
----------------------------------------------
When a grammar is recursive (e.g. expressions containing sub-expressions),
you need a forward reference:
 
    let ParseExpr, eref = createParserForwardedToRef<expr>()
    // define parseExpr using ParseExpr for the recursive call
    do eref := parseExpr   // wire it up at the end
 
ParseExpr is the parser you use inside other parsers.
eref is the ref cell you assign the real implementation to once it is defined.
Do not forget the "do eref := ..." line or the parser will loop or fail at runtime.
 
 
TYPICAL EXAM PATTERN - SECTION 4.5
------------------------------------
Every exam asks you to parse an instruction set or expression language.
The pattern is always:
 
    // 1. Parse a keyword, discard it, then parse the argument(s)
    let parseSet =
        pstring "SET" >>. pws1 >>. pReg .>> pws1 .>>. pint32
        |>> (fun (r, v) -> Set(r, v))
 
    // 2. Combine alternatives with choice
    let parseInstr = choice [parseSet; parseCopy; parseInc; ...]
 
The 2022 exam (stack machine) also shows parsing a sequence of instructions
separated by newlines using a forwarded reference, which is harder:
 
    let programParse, pref = createParserForwardedToRef<stackProgram>()
    let singleCmd = ...
    let seqCmd    = cmdParse .>> pcmdsep .>>. programParse
                    |>> (fun (c, cs) -> c :: cs)
    do pref := choice [singleCmd; seqCmd]
 
 
COMMON MISTAKES
---------------
- Using >> (Haskell-style) instead of >>.  JParsec uses .>> and .>>.
- Forgetting "do eref := ..." at the bottom. The parser silently loops.
- Wrong associativity when chaining .>>. - it produces nested tuples.
  Use pattern matching in |>> to destructure, e.g. |>> (fun (a, b) -> ...).
- Using <|> when two alternatives share a prefix. JParsec has no backtracking
  by default, so if p1 partially consumes input and then fails, p2 never runs.
  Order your choice list so more specific parsers come first.
